==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/between_set_yes_rate_sd.py <==
# description: Standard deviation, across example sets with at least 20 responses, of the per-set yes-rate; observed above the null means the model makes sets too similar in overall generality (under-produces set-to-set differences in concept breadth), below means it exaggerates them.
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/far_target_yes_rate.py <==
# description: Proportion of "yes" for targets more than 40 away from every example; observed above the model's null means the model under-produces broad/random endorsement of distant targets (e.g. missing lapse or very broad concepts), below means it over-produces it.
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/lag1_response_agreement.py <==
# description: Proportion of consecutive rows (same session: trial_index increases by 1) whose yes/no responses agree, minus the agreement expected from the two rows' marginal yes-rate; the model treats responses as independent, so observed above the null means humans perseverate on their previous answer (below: alternate).
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/near_minus_far_gradient.py <==
# description: Yes-rate for non-example targets within 3 of the nearest example minus yes-rate for targets more than 40 away; observed below the null means the model's generalisation gradient over numerical distance is too steep (humans are flatter), above means too shallow.
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/parity_match_effect.py <==
# description: Among multi-example sets whose examples all share a parity, and non-example targets, yes-rate when the target shares that parity minus when it does not; observed below the null means the model over-weights parity (even/odd rules) relative to humans, above means it under-weights it.
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/recency_order_effect.py <==
# description: For sets of 2+ examples and non-example targets, yes-rate when the target is strictly closer to the last-shown example than to the first-shown one minus the reverse; the model is order-invariant, so observed above the null means humans show recency (weight the last example more), below means primacy.
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/target_in_set_yes_rate.py <==
# description: Proportion of "yes" when the target is one of the shown examples; observed below the model's null means the model over-predicts endorsement of already-seen examples (humans lapse/hedge even on members), above means it under-predicts it.
==> iter_0/critique/test_stats/trial_index_drift.py <==
# description: Yes-rate in the first half of the session (trial_index below median) minus yes-rate in the second half; the model has no time dependence, so observed above the null means humans become more conservative over the session in a way the model does not produce (below: more liberal).
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/above_vs_below_span_asymmetry.py <==
# description: Yes rate for targets 1-15 above the set max minus yes rate for targets 1-15 below the set min; model intervals are width-only (symmetric), so observed != null_mean shows a directional (upward vs downward) generalization bias the model misses.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/far_target_yes_rate.py <==
# description: Yes rate for targets at least 30 away from every shown example; the model has no lapse/guessing term, so observed above null_mean means people say yes to distant targets more than the model allows.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/in_set_target_yes_rate.py <==
# description: Yes rate when the target is one of the shown examples; observed below null_mean means people reject exact examples more often than the model (which predicts ~1) allows.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/last_vs_first_example_proximity.py <==
# description: Among multi-example sets, targets not in set and within 15 of exactly one of the first/last example: yes rate (near last shown) minus yes rate (near first shown); model is order-invariant, so observed > null_mean means recency-weighted generalization.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/near_span_decay_slope.py <==
# description: Among targets outside the examples range and within 40 of it, OLS slope of yes on distance-to-range (per 10 units); observed less negative than null_mean means people generalize to nearby numbers more gradually (flatter) than the model, more negative means a sharper fall-off.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/parity_match_effect.py <==
# description: For sets whose examples all share one parity, among non-example targets within the examples range +/-10: yes rate for same-parity targets minus opposite-parity targets; observed > null_mean means people use parity more than the model, < means less.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/trial_index_yes_slope.py <==
# description: OLS slope of yes response on trial_index (per 100 trials); model has no time dependence, so a nonzero observed slope vs null_mean ~0 indicates drift in yes-bias over the session.
==> iter_1/critique/test_stats/wide_span_interior_yes_rate.py <==
# description: Yes rate for non-example targets strictly inside the examples range when the range is >=40 wide; observed below null_mean means the model over-fills wide spans with interval hypotheses (people treat sparse wide sets as rule-like, not as ranges).
